I just read, on de.comp.os.linux, that the DFN RPC package is available
for Linux.  Here's the de-htmlized version of the README file in
the appropriate directory.

                 THE DFN REMOTE PROCEDURE CALL TOOL (DFN-RPC)
                                       =

   =

   =

   The DFN-RPC, a Remote Procedure Call Tool, was developed to distribute=

   and parallelize scientific-technical application programs between a
   workstation and a compute server or a cluster. All processes will run
   under user-account. The interface is optimized for applications
   written in fortran, but the DFN-RPC can also be used in a C
   environment.
   =

   The new release 1.0.35 has a lot of features for distributing and
   parallelizing application programs:
     * synchronous, asynchronous and parallel RPCs,
     * datapipes for message passing,
     * a central waiting point for the finish of a parallel RPC, the
       recept of a datapipe message, an X-event, an input at stdin or an
       other event on a Unix filedescriptor,
     * quickstart routines to build a network of parallel application
       processes,
     * a graphical monitor,
     * a remote file access library,
     * and a lot of examples.
       =

   =

   =

   In the moment the documentation has two parts
     * dfnrpc_news_35.ps describes the new features in Rel. 1.0.35 [4a].
     * dfnrpc.ps describes the basic features of the DFN-RPC 1.0 [4].
       =

   =

   =

   If the DFN-RPC is not installed in your local environment then you can=

   get the tar-File dfn_rpc_1.0.35.tar.Z (2.3 MByte)
