	Some of you may remember the bogoboost patch -- a patch which
would change setup.S to alter the DRAM refresh timer before switching
to protected mode. The idea is that on older machines, you could get up to
a 5% performance increase by extending the period of the refresh
timer from the standard 15us. (Most newer machines implement a hidden 
or slow (120us) refresh in the BIOS setup menu, and hence already take 
advantage of this boost.)

	If you don't remember the bogoboost patch, then I have included
the README file after the LSM entry so you can get a better idea of
just what it is that is being discussed here.

	The scoop is that now you *don't* have to patch your kernel
to do the same thing! You can use this program --  "dram" -- to do
the job! That means you can put it into one of your /etc/rc files,
and have it set the refreh rate every time automatically. No patching,
no mess, no fuss. :-)

	There is a man page which contains most (but not all) of the 
information in the README file. The package is dram-1.00.tar.gz, which
will be temporarily in /pub/Linux/Incoming on sunsite, before moving
to /pub/Linux/system/Misc

Paul.
